---
title: Document your API with Doxygen
description: Generate and inspect HTML and XML documentation for a C or C++ project.
section: How-to guides
order: 8
updated: '2026-10-14'
---

Use this guide to generate an API reference from a C or C++ project. You need an existing project and Doxygen on `PATH`; check with `doxygen --version`. On macOS, install it with `brew install doxygen` ([formula](https://formulae.brew.sh/formula/doxygen)); other platforms can follow the [installation guide](https://www.doxygen.nl/manual/install.html).

## 1. Document the public interface

In a Rivet project, run `:RivetHeaderDefaults` and choose **Doxygen documentation** for future files. Use `:RivetHeader` to add it to an existing header or module interface. See [file comment headers](/docs/comment-headers/) for the commands.

Add API comments beside public declarations. For example, document a public addition function:

```cpp
/**
 * @brief Add two integers.
 * @param a First operand.
 * @param b Second operand.
 * @return Their sum, which must be representable as an int.
 */
int add(int a, int b);
```

Document containing public namespaces too, for example `/** @brief Public engine API. */` above `export namespace engine`. Include caller-visible assumptions and error behavior. Keep one canonical description beside the public declaration. See Doxygen's [comment conventions](https://www.doxygen.nl/manual/docblocks.html).

## 2. Configure the inputs and outputs

From the project root, generate a configuration if you do not already have one:

```sh
doxygen -g Doxyfile
```

Edit these entries. This example targets Rivet's C++ feature layout:

```ini
PROJECT_NAME           = engine
OUTPUT_DIRECTORY       = build/docs
INPUT                  = src app README.md
FILE_PATTERNS          = *.h *.hpp *.c *.cpp *.cppm *.md *.dox
RECURSIVE              = YES
EXTRACT_ALL            = NO
SOURCE_BROWSER         = YES
GENERATE_HTML          = YES
GENERATE_XML           = YES
GENERATE_LATEX         = NO
```

Set `PROJECT_NAME` to your project name and list only existing source directories in `INPUT`. Add `include` for a public-header layout. For a flat layout, use `INPUT = .` and `EXCLUDE = build .cache .git`. Keep `EXTRACT_ALL = NO` to retain undocumented-member warnings. See the [configuration reference](https://www.doxygen.nl/manual/config.html).

## 3. Generate and inspect the reference

```sh
mkdir -p build
doxygen Doxyfile
```

Open `build/docs/html/index.html`, resolve relevant warnings, and confirm your public functions, types, and module interfaces appear. Doxygen recognizes `.cppm`, but its parser output still needs inspection. The [getting-started guide](https://www.doxygen.nl/manual/starting.html) covers the generation process.

Commit `Doxyfile` and your documentation comments. Generated projects ignore `build/`, so the rendered output stays out of source control.

## Add an optional CMake documentation target

To regenerate documentation through CMake, add this after the generated includes in the editable root `CMakeLists.txt`:

```cmake
find_package(Doxygen QUIET)
if(DOXYGEN_FOUND)
  add_custom_target(docs
    COMMAND "${DOXYGEN_EXECUTABLE}" "${PROJECT_SOURCE_DIR}/Doxyfile"
    WORKING_DIRECTORY "${PROJECT_SOURCE_DIR}"
    VERBATIM)
endif()
```

Configure again, then build the target:

```sh
cmake --preset debug
cmake --build --preset debug --target docs
```

Use your own preset names in an existing project. The target runs only when requested. [Project upgrades](/docs/upgrading/) preserve locally extended root CMake files.

## Connect a documentation project

For an external renderer, pass the complete `build/docs/xml/` directory, including `index.xml` and the compound files, together with the source revision. Use a Doxygen version supported by the consumer and inspect its rendered API before publishing.

To link two Doxygen sites, add `GENERATE_TAGFILE = build/docs/engine.tag` to the producer's configuration and regenerate. Set `TAGFILES = path/to/engine.tag=https://your-docs-host/engine/` in the consumer's configuration. Replace the path and URL with the producer's tag file and published HTML location. Check that expected namespaces and symbols are present in the tag file. See [external documentation links](https://www.doxygen.nl/manual/external.html).
